            <ArticleTitle>Asthma symptoms associated with depression and lower quality of life: a population survey.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>437-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify any association between asthma and depression and quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">A face-to-face Health Omnibus Survey of a random and representative sample of the South Australian population in August 1998.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">3010 randomly selected participants aged 15 years and over.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prevalence of doctor-diagnosed asthma, and scores for depression (measured by PRIME-MD instrument) and quality of life (measured by SF-36) in affected participants.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The prevalence of asthma was 9.9%. The prevalence of major depression was significantly higher for those who experienced dyspnoea, wakening at night with asthma, and morning symptoms of asthma. Quality-of-life scores were also lower for the same groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Depression is a serious but potentially remediable comorbidity with asthma that may affect appropriate diagnosis and outcome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
